\import{macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{frap}

\title{Five encounters with a cube}

\p{Put a solved three-layer cube on a table and turn its right face once.
The move is familiar, but it raises concrete puzzles: which labelled pieces
moved, why can a visually plausible arrangement be impossible, and how can
another person undo the turn without watching the first hand? Begin with
these experiences, then introduce only the distinctions needed to explain
them.}

\subtree{
  \title{One turn is larger than the visible face}

  \tikzfig{\begin{tikzpicture}[
    moved/.style={fill=gray!25},
    arrow/.style={-{Stealth[length=2mm]},semithick}
  ]
  \node at (1.5,3.65) {before};
  \draw (0,0) grid (3,3);
  \node at (1.5,-0.35) {front face};
  \node at (7.5,3.65) {after $R$};
  \draw (6,0) grid (9,3);
  \fill[moved] (8,0) rectangle (9,3);
  \draw (6,0) grid (9,3);
  \node at (7.5,-0.35) {moved front column};
  \draw[arrow] (3.6,1.5) -- node[above] {turn one layer} (5.4,1.5);
  \end{tikzpicture}}

  \p{The shaded strip is only the visible part of the layer: the same
  stickers cross the front, right, back, top, and bottom faces; viewed from
  above, the top-face part is the corresponding rightmost strip. A whole-cube
  rotation would change the viewing frame, whereas this face move keeps the
  six centres fixed and changes one layer. The [layered-cube family](frap-0003)
  makes this distinction for every #{N}; the [complete three-layer
  cube](frap-0016) spells it out for the six outer generators.}
}

\subtree{
  \title{A sticker needs a home and a frame}

  \transclude{frap-001D}

  \tikzfig{\begin{tikzpicture}[
    box/.style={draw,rounded corners=2pt,align=center,text width=34mm,
      minimum height=11mm,font=\small},
    arrow/.style={-{Stealth[length=2mm]},semithick},
    lab/.style={font=\scriptsize,fill=white,inner sep=1pt}
  ]
    \node[box] (a) at (-2.9,1.0) {54 facet-incidence\\ positions};
    \node[box] (b) at (2.9,1.0) {corner and edge\\ sticker fibres\\ plus six centres};
    \node[box] (c) at (0,-1.35) {$(\sigma,x;\tau,z)$\\ fixed-frame state};
    \draw[arrow] (a) -- node[lab,above,yshift=10pt] {group by cubie} (b);
    \draw[arrow] (b) -- node[lab,right,xshift=16pt] {record destination + frame} (c);
  \end{tikzpicture}}

  \p{A player sees colours; a proof must also track each sticker's
  \vocabk{cubie}{frap-001D} and transported
  \vocabk{fixed local frame}{frap-001D}. [How the cube models connect](frap-001A)
  follows one sticker through incidence position, cubie fibre, and fixed-frame
  coordinate. A colour quotient forgets labels, while a larger cube adds
  positions, so the state space changes with the question.}
}

\subtree{
  \title{A plausible state can still be impossible}

  \p{Take a solved cube and twist one corner in place. The resulting
  \vocabk{sticker state}{frap-001D} still puts every sticker in a cubie-sized
  home, and the three colours at that corner look locally consistent. But the
  corner orientations now have sum #{1} modulo #{3},
  whereas every face turn preserves sum #{0}. No sequence of legal turns can
  produce this state.}

  \tikzfig{\begin{tikzpicture}[
    sticker/.style={draw,minimum size=9mm,inner sep=1pt},
    arrow/.style={-{Stealth[length=2mm]},semithick}
  ]
  \node[sticker] (u) at (0,1.25) {$U$};
  \node[sticker] (f) at (-1.1,-0.65) {$F$};
  \node[sticker] (r) at (1.1,-0.65) {$R$};
  \draw[arrow] (u) to[bend right=18] node[left] {$+1$} (f);
  \draw[arrow] (f) to[bend right=18] node[below] {$+1$} (r);
  \draw[arrow] (r) to[bend right=18] node[right] {$+1$} (u);
  \end{tikzpicture}}

  \p{The [complete three-layer cube](frap-0016) derives the matching edge
  and permutation restrictions from the six generators. The calculation is
  useful at the table: it tells a solver when a scrambled-looking object was
  assembled illegally, and it tells a proof which state space is worth
  searching.}

  \p{A human algorithm has to preserve these legal states while it borrows
  pieces from completed areas.}

  \tikzfig{\begin{tikzpicture}[
    stagebox/.style={draw,rounded corners=2pt,align=center,text width=25mm,
      minimum height=10mm,font=\small},
    arrow/.style={-{Stealth[length=2mm]},semithick}
  ]
    \node[stagebox] (a) at (-2.1,1.35) {recognize\\ the case};
    \node[stagebox] (b) at (2.1,1.35) {setup\\ the slot};
    \node[stagebox] (c) at (2.1,-1.35) {trigger\\ $T=R'D'RD$};
    \node[stagebox] (d) at (-2.1,-1.35) {undo setup\\ and inspect};
    \draw[arrow] (a) -- (b);
    \draw[arrow] (b) -- (c);
    \draw[arrow] (c) -- (d);
  \end{tikzpicture}}

  \p{A formula often disturbs pieces that were already placed. The disturbance
  is acceptable only when the setup is undone and the completed pieces return.
  A working slot, a trigger, and an inverse setup make that promise visible.
  The [complete layer-first method](frap-0017) repeats this pattern through
  the cross, the first layer, the middle layer, and the last layer. Its
  completeness depends on the preservation and termination checks, not on
  the formula's familiarity.}
}

\subtree{
  \title{Undoing a scramble checks the notation}

  \tikzfig{\begin{tikzpicture}[
    box/.style={draw,rounded corners=2pt,align=center,text width=31mm,
      minimum height=11mm,font=\small},
    arrow/.style={-{Stealth[length=2mm]},semithick},
    lab/.style={font=\scriptsize,fill=white,inner sep=1pt}
  ]
  \node[box] (a) at (0,0) {solved state};
  \node[box] (b) at (5,0) {$R U R' U' F R U R' U' F'$};
  \node[box] (c) at (10,0) {scrambled state};
  \draw[arrow] (a) -- node[lab,above] {scramble} (b);
  \draw[arrow] (b) -- (c);
  \draw[arrow] (c.south) .. controls +(0,-1.2) and +(0,-1.2) ..
    node[lab,below] {inverse replay} (a.south);
  \end{tikzpicture}}

  \p{The [worked replay and projected lower bound](frap-001B) fixes the viewing convention,
  records the ten tokens, and applies their inverse. Sticker replay returns
  to solved. A corner-only projected search finds no solution of depth at
  most two, so the evidence is the bounded statement}
  ##{3\le d_{\rm HTM}\le 10.}
  \p{The inverse word is an upper-bound witness; the projected search is a
  lower-bound witness. The [computer account](frap-0018) explains why these
  checks do not prove that ten is shortest. A state, a word, and a replay
  log are enough to test the notation before asking for a general solver.}
}

\subtree{
  \title{Changing one ingredient changes the problem}

  \p{Once the move, its frame, its local repair, and its replay are explicit,
  three variations separate the effects of enlarging the cube, changing the
  shape, and forgetting labels.}

  \tikzfig{\begin{tikzpicture}[
    box/.style={draw,rounded corners=2pt,align=center,text width=33mm,
      minimum height=10mm,font=\small},
    arrow/.style={-{Stealth[length=2mm]},semithick}
  ]
  \node[box] (root) at (0,0) {one fixed-frame\\ face turn};
  \node[box] (n) at (5,1.8) {larger $N$: which\\ position orbits appear?};
  \node[box] (p) at (5,0) {another shape: which\\ facet moves exist?};
  \node[box] (q) at (5,-1.8) {a quotient: which\\ observations inherit moves?};
  \draw[arrow] (root) -- (n);
  \draw[arrow] (root) -- (p);
  \draw[arrow] (root) -- (q);
  \end{tikzpicture}}

  \p{The [layered-cube family](frap-0003) develops the first variation; the
  incidence and facet construction in [incidence, symmetry and moves](frap-0002)
  develops the second; and [which moves survive a quotient?](frap-0019)
  gives the descent condition for the third. [Framed orientations](frap-000A),
  [label transport and topology](frap-0014), and [scrambling, spectra and
  search](frap-0015) then study the extra information each variation
  introduces or forgets. Their hypotheses stay attached to the concrete move
  that prompted the question.}
}

\p{A face turn can therefore be drawn, named, replayed, and checked before
it is generalized. The resulting state maps and invariants support later
formal certification or measured comparisons, while the examples here make
clear which claim each later programme would have to establish.}
